        "math_academy_id": "q-340337",
        "problem": "Given that $y=\\frac{x}{cos\u2061x}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}\u2061x}\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}\n$).$",
        "worked_solution": "Let $u=x$ and $v=cos\u2061x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-sin\u2061x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}-u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{cos\u2061x\\cdot 1-x\\cdot (-sin\u2061x)}{{cos}^{2}\u2061x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}\u2061x}\\cdot (cos\u2061x+xsin\u2061x\\,).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "actual_result": "Correct",
        "deliberately_incorrect_submission": false,
